What this covers
----------------

The explanatory and user-facing writing, wherever it lives in the tree:

  * README.md and the other Markdown documents
  * the prose rendered by the /sources, /terms and /privacy pages
  * the user-facing strings in src/lib/c3/facts.ts (the interview questions and
    the "why we are asking" text), src/lib/c3/documents.ts (the suggested
    documents), and src/lib/c3/advisory.ts (the uncertainty flags and advisories)
  * the plain-language explanations in the rule traces

This is deliberate. The hard part of this project is not the fold in
classify.ts, it is working out how to explain paragraph 3(1)(q) to someone who
has to write a cover letter about it. A legal clinic, a community forum, or
another tool should be able to reuse that writing with the least possible
friction, and a software licence is an awkward instrument for prose.


